ate question from how the system should respond when someone breaks the law, so both are `adjacent`.\n- **The policy pages that do touch the question aren't his own words.** They are campaign pages written in the third person (\"Steve will work to create new sentencing enhancements\"). Under V1 that makes them `third-party-characterization`, which can't support a chair.\n  - **Masked criminals:** this page adds a sentencing enhancement for one aggravating factor. Even read as his own words, the annex \"One offense\" rule caps it at `direction-only`. It shows the side of real consequences but doesn't separate \"accountability is the priority\" from \"the toughest penalties the law allows\".\n  - **Prop 36 line (small-business page):** it covers retail theft only and mixes \"enforcement and treatment\", so it is also `direction-only`.\n- **The rest are adjacent or rhetorical.** The CHP expansion is policing, which has its own topic. The crypto-fraud and gun-rights lines name no general approach to punishment.\n\n**Two calls a reviewer may want to check:**\n- **Undated pages:** the campaign pages carry no date, so I left `date` null but coded them `in-term`, since they are the live site of the current campaign. Strictly, an undated statement could have been coded `undated`; the row is blank either way.\n- **Quotes:** I recorded three. The masked-mask prison time line and the Prop 36 line are tagged `lever`. The debate line about removing dangerous criminals is tagged `direction` and not quotable (`off-question`).\n\n**Needs source:** a first-person statement from Hilton (questionnaire, debate answer, or a first-person issue page) giving his general view on punishment versus treatment or restitution.\n\nFile: `backend/data/stance-research/2026-10-07-shadow-hilton-judicial-criminal-justice/labels/coder-1.json`\n\n**Connectors:** Benevity, Candid, Canva, Figma, Harvey and WorkOS need authorizing in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. The GitHub plug